---GitHub Actions OIDC claims to match against. Each field is a regular expression.
---The `sub` claim is required; all other claims are optional. A token matches only when
---all provided patterns match simultaneously (AND semantics).
---@class datadog.v2.GitHubOIDCClaimPatterns
---@field actor? string Regular expression matched against the `actor` claim.
---@field actor_id? string Regular expression matched against the `actor_id` claim.
---@field enterprise? string Regular expression matched against the `enterprise` claim.
---@field enterprise_id? string Regular expression matched against the `enterprise_id` claim.
---@field environment? string Regular expression matched against the `environment` claim.
---@field event_name? string Regular expression matched against the `event_name` claim.
---@field job_workflow_ref? string Regular expression matched against the `job_workflow_ref` claim.
---@field ref? string Regular expression matched against the `ref` claim.
---@field ref_type? string Regular expression matched against the `ref_type` claim.
---@field repository? string Regular expression matched against the `repository` claim.
---@field repository_id? string Regular expression matched against the `repository_id` claim.
---@field repository_owner? string Regular expression matched against the `repository_owner` claim.
---@field repository_owner_id? string Regular expression matched against the `repository_owner_id` claim.
---@field repository_visibility? string Regular expression matched against the `repository_visibility` claim.
---@field runner_environment? string Regular expression matched against the `runner_environment` claim.
---@field sub string Regular expression matched against the entire `sub` (subject) claim, the primary GitHub Actions OIDC identifier (for example, `repo:<OWNER>/<REPO>:ref:refs/heads/main`). The pattern must begin with `repo:<OWNER>/`, where `<OWNER>` is a literal repository-owner name rather than a regular expression.
---@field workflow? string Regular expression matched against the `workflow` claim.
---@field workflow_ref? string Regular expression matched against the `workflow_ref` claim.

---Trigger a workflow from a GitHub webhook. To trigger a workflow from GitHub, you must set a `webhookSecret`. In your GitHub Webhook Settings, set the Payload URL to "base_url"/api/v2/workflows/"workflow_id"/webhook?orgId="org_id", select application/json for the content type, and be highly recommend enabling SSL verification for security. The workflow must be published.
---@class datadog.v2.GithubWebhookTrigger
---@field rateLimit? datadog.v2.TriggerRateLimit

---Schema for a GitHub webhook-based trigger.
---@class datadog.v2.GithubWebhookTriggerWrapper
---@field githubWebhookTrigger datadog.v2.GithubWebhookTrigger
