---@class datadog.v2.ListTagConfigurationsRequest
---@field filter_configured_? boolean Only return custom metrics that have been configured (`true`) or not configured (`false`) with Metrics Without Limits.
---@field filter_is_configurable_? boolean Only return metrics that are eligible (`true`) or ineligible (`false`) for configuration with Metrics Without Limits.
---@field filter_tags_configured_? string Only return metrics that have the given tag key(s) in their Metrics Without Limits configuration (included or excluded).
---@field filter_metric_type_? datadog.v2.MetricTagConfigurationMetricTypeCategory|string Only return metrics of the given metric type. If omitted, metrics of all types are returned. `distribution` also matches OpenTelemetry histograms; use `exponential_histogram` or `explicit_histogram` to return only one kind of OpenTelemetry histogram.
---@field filter_include_percentiles_? boolean Only return distribution metrics that have percentile aggregations enabled (true) or disabled (false).
---@field filter_queried_? boolean Only return metrics that have been queried (true) or not queried (false) in the look back window. Set the window with `filter[queried][window][seconds]`; if omitted, a default window is used.
---@field filter_queried__window__seconds_? number The look-back window for `filter[queried]`, in seconds. You must also set `filter[queried]`, or the request returns a 400 error. The default value is 2,592,000 seconds (30 days), the maximum value is 15,552,000 seconds (180 days), and the minimum value is 0. With `0`, the window ends now, so `filter[queried]=true` returns almost no metrics and `filter[queried]=false` returns all metrics. For example: `filter[queried]=true&filter[queried][window][seconds]=604800`.
---@field filter_tags_? string Only return metrics that were submitted with tags matching this expression. You can use AND, OR, IN, and wildcards. For example: `filter[tags]=env IN (staging,test) AND service:web*`.
---@field filter_related_assets_? boolean Only return metrics that are used in at least one dashboard, monitor, notebook, or SLO.
---@field filter_metrics_id_? string Only return metrics whose name matches this value. A value without `*` matches metric names that contain it, ignoring case (for example, `http.request` matches `app.HTTP.request.count`). A full metric name also matches longer names that contain it. `*` is a wildcard that can appear anywhere and more than once, and these patterns also ignore case: `*.request.count` matches names that end with `.request.count`, and `app.*.count` matches names that start with `app.` and end with `.count`. The exception is a value whose only `*` is at the end (for example, `http.request*`): it matches names that start with that text and is case-sensitive. Spaces are treated as `*`. Only one value is accepted; repeating the parameter returns `400 Bad Request`. Allowed characters are letters, digits, `_`, `.`, `*`, and spaces. An empty value is ignored.
---@field filter_historical_metrics_? boolean Only return metrics that have historical metrics ingestion enabled (`true`) or not enabled (`false`).
---@field include? string Include related resources in the response. Set to `metric_volumes` to include indexed and ingested volume counts for each metric.
---@field sort? string Sort results by metric volume. Prefix a key with `-` for descending order. Supported keys: `metric_volumes.indexed_volume`, `metric_volumes.ingested_volume`, `metric_volumes.indexed_volume_delta`, `metric_volumes.ingested_volume_delta`. Requires a paginated request (`page[size]` or `page[cursor]`).
---@field window_seconds_? number Only return metrics that have been actively reporting in this window. The default value is 3,600 seconds (1 hour), the maximum value is 5,184,000 seconds (60 days), and the minimum value is 0. With `0`, the API returns only metrics that reported in the last few minutes.
---@field page_size_? number Maximum number of results per page. Always send it: without `page[size]` or `page[cursor]`, the response is not paginated and is capped at 250,000 metrics by default, with no indication when results are left out. Send `page[size]` on the first request to opt in to pagination. On subsequent requests, send `page[cursor]` set to the value of `meta.pagination.next_cursor` from the previous response. To retain a custom page size, include `page[size]` on every request. The default value is 10000, the maximum value is 10000, and the minimum value is 1.
---@field page_cursor_? string Cursor for pagination. Use `page[size]` to opt-in to pagination and get the first page; for subsequent pages, use the value from `meta.pagination.next_cursor` in the response. Pagination is complete when `next_cursor` is null. An expired cursor returns `410 Gone`; restart from the first page without `page[cursor]`.

---@class datadog.v2.ListTagIndexingRuleExemptionsRequest
---@field filter_metric_prefix_? string Filter exemptions to metrics whose names start with this literal prefix. Matching is case-sensitive. Wildcard characters are treated literally.
---@field filter_safe_? boolean Filter exemptions by whether they are safe to remove. An exemption is considered safe when a fresh, usable estimate indicates that removing it would not increase indexed volume and would not drop tags used to query the metric in monitors, dashboards, notebooks, or SLOs. Set to `true` to return safe-to-remove exemptions, or `false` to return all other exemptions, including those with unavailable or stale estimates.
---@field filter_tag_indexing_rule_id_? string Filter exemptions to metrics matched by the tag indexing rule with this UUID.
---@field sort? datadog.v2.TagIndexingRuleExemptionSort|string Sort field. Prefix the field with `-` for descending order. The default is `estimated_change_in_volume` in ascending order, with the largest reductions (most negative values) first.
---@field page_size_? number Maximum number of results per page. Defaults to 10000; accepts values from 1 to 10000. If `meta.pagination.next_cursor` is non-null, request the next page by passing that value as `page[cursor]`. Continue until `next_cursor` is null.
---@field page_cursor_? string Cursor for pagination. Use `page[size]` to opt-in to pagination and get the first page; for subsequent pages, use the value from `meta.pagination.next_cursor` in the response. Pagination is complete when `next_cursor` is null.

